<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Cascade Engineering — First Day</title>
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9011-new-start-different-company-earned-experience-clear-eyed</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      Cascade Engineering's test facility is planetside — an unusual choice
      for a propulsion contractor, but Cascade does unusual things and is
      proud of it. The air smells like actual air: grass, humidity, a faint
      trace of jet fuel from the adjacent aerospace strip. The building is
      low, wide, concrete and glass. There are windows everywhere. Through
      one of them, visible from the orientation room, is a mountain range
      that has no rubble in it, only peaks. The coffee is mediocre, which
      feels honest.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      Cascade Engineering is smaller than Helix Dynamics — 3,000 employees,
      three facilities, no orbital yards. They do advanced propulsion research
      and prototype qualification. Their clients include three colonial
      governments and two independent exploration consortia. Their safety
      record is the best in the sector. It is also why they can attract
      engineers who left somewhere else for principled reasons. The hire
      manager mentioned this, briefly and without elaboration, during your
      final interview. You understood.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      Orientation room, Building C. A window with a mountain range in it.
      A room full of people starting new things. Different from the last
      time you sat in an orientation room — you are not nervous in the
      same way. You are something more useful than not-nervous: you are
      clear on what you're doing and why.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      You are starting again, at a different company, with a record that
      includes a completed qualification programme, a formal IESB technical
      consultation, and — depending on which path brought you here —
      either a commendation in your file or a gap in your employment
      history that you've explained, simply and truthfully, in every
      interview. Cascade Engineering hired you anyway. The Lead Engineer
      here is named Marcus Osei. He was the external reviewer on the
      Perseverance incident report. He knows your name. He asked for
      you specifically. You only found that out after you accepted the
      offer.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Marcus Osei: Lead Engineer, Cascade Engineering. 40s, deliberate,
      the kind of person who pauses before speaking and means it. He has
      read everything. He has not brought any of it up yet. He is letting
      you have a first day. When he does bring it up — and he will — it
      will be to ask what you learned, not to relitigate it.

      The Orientation Cohort: Six engineers. Nervous, some of them.
      Over-prepared, one of them. You recognise the type. You were
      that type once. You are going to be a good colleague to them.

      Tomás Engel: Texted you this morning: "good luck. let me know
      how the coffee is." You texted back: "mediocre. feels right."
      He sent a thumbs up. That's enough.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Orientation Room, Building C, Cascade Engineering, Kepler Station Surface Facility</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        New hire, Senior Test Engineer. First day. Different company.
        Lighter than you were. Clearer than you were. Ready for the work.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        This is what's on the other side of it. Not triumph — just
        the next thing, done well, with better information than you
        started with. The mountain range through the window is not
        going anywhere. The coffee will not improve. You have a notebook,
        a badge that works, and a first day. That's more than enough
        to begin.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is the epilogue world — convergence point for multiple paths.
      The tone is earned-quiet optimism. Not everything was resolved.
      The Mjolnir investigation may still be open. Vasquez's fate may
      be uncertain. Sato may not have faced consequences yet. The player
      carries that. But they are also here, starting something new,
      and that is genuinely good. The mountains matter. Let the
      ordinary details do the work. This world should feel like
      a deep breath. No crises. No decisions with major consequences.
      Just: the beginning of a thing, with the full weight of what
      came before it, and the knowledge that the player — for all
      their paths — did the work with integrity. That's the ending.
      It doesn't need to be louder than that.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
